WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.680
Artists like to share stories about sparks
of inspiration that motivate them to create.

00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:09.350
When it comes to HBO's new adaptation of His
Dark Materials, that spark happens to be one

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.000
very distressing flight.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.280
In an interview with The Los Angeles Times,
His Dark Materials executive producer Jane

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.529
Tranter revealed that a terrifying experience
while flying was behind her push to bring

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.020
Philip Pullman's popular fantasy novel series
to the small screen.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.380
The incident happened around five years ago
while Tranter was on a flight from Los Angeles

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.060
to New York.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.960
During the flight, a silence fell over the
passengers as the plane began to quickly lose

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:32.840
altitude.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.960
As Tranter tells it, the pilots jumped into
action and prepared to make an emergency landing

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.880
in Chicago.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.920
Tranter became emotionally overwhelmed, too
afraid to finish the work she was doing.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.020
So she turned to her Kindle instead.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.540
To comfort herself, Tranter began reading
the end of Pullman's The Amber Spyglass, the

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.740
final book in the author's best-selling His
Dark Materials trilogy.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.560
Tranter told the LA Times:

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.720
"The books are so spiritual, and they have
got so much to say about our human mortality

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.580
and what death means and separation.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.040
It took me to another world."

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.680
The experience convinced Tranter to follow
up on the rights of Pullman's books, a curiosity

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.460
that led her straight into the offices of
New Line Cinema.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.140
The studio had already attempted to adapt
the books to film in 2007, but ultimately

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.979
dropped those plans after the first installment,
The Golden Compass, underperformed both critically

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.500
and at the box office.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.159
The Twilight Saga: New Moon director Chris
Weitz helmed and wrote that particular Pullman

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:29.530
adaptation, which cost the studio $180 million
to make but only earned around $70 million

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:34.100
domestically, with most of its box office
takings coming from overseas.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.260
The Golden Compass also faced hurdles beyond
its inability to draw a crowd.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.440
For example, Pullman's books were fiercely
critical of both the Catholic Church and Christianity

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.200
in general.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.840
"Philip Pullman is a known atheist.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.040
He's been known to be pushing on kids for
a long time.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.380
He put out a DVD, for example, in England,
that says, 'Why atheist?'"

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.440
As a result, they became the subject of intense
debate and scrutiny by some religious audiences,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.700
and the 2007 movie was no different.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.080
The story was so controversial that, in the
year after the film's shaky release, The Golden

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.800
Compass landed in the American Library Association's
list of top 10 books that individuals and

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.080
organizations had attempted to have banned
in the United States.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.440
New Line attempted to curb some of this reaction
to the film by watering down the film's religious

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.720
content, but other critics and audiences seemed
to think that decision hurt more than it helped

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.560
the narrative.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.040
Despite a less-than-stellar box office turnout
and the controversy surrounding the original

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.660
movie, Tranter convinced studio executives
that Pullman's fantasy could live again on

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.680
the small screen.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.760
She and the rest of the His Dark Materials
team were able to persuade not only New Line

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.310
executives that the television adaptation
was worth the risk, but also several high-profile

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.880
stars including James McAvoy, Ruth Wilson,
and Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.690
It's a win Tranter attributed to another popular
HBO adaptation, which helped make the case

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.860
that an elaborate fantasy like Pullman's could
both be done and be well-received.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:54.960
Tranter explained:

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.620
"Game of Thrones really did that job for me."

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.180
Not many movies that struggle at the box office
get a second chance on the small screen, and

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.350
Tranter took a risk with His Dark Materials
but it seems that her leap of faith has paid

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:11.060
off in kind, as response to the small-screen
adaptation has been stellar so far.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:16.160
Holding an 84 percent critical rating and
a 92 percent audience score on Rotten Tomatoes,

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.700
His Dark Materials is being praised for its
impressive performances, strong visuals and

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.780
its ability to capture the spirit of Pullman's
original novels.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.860
One critic even wrote that the show is:

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.500
"...proof that TV is now the best medium for
bringing epic literary fantasy to the screen."

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.800
Almost makes that death-defying flight worth
it, don't you think?

